From: Proton CRM <noreply@proton.local>
Subject: [PATCH] P9 tasks 2/3/6: new-inbound alerting in the main Chatwoot UI

Moves the three working alert primitives (toast, sound, desktop notification)
out of the my-tasks dashboard-app iframe and into the Chatwoot bundle, wires
them to new inbound customer messages, and adds the per-agent preferences page
that makes them configurable. Backs features/alerts/rules_store.py and
features/alerts/rules_router.py.

NOTHING IS REIMPLEMENTED, AND THE my-tasks APP IS NOT TOUCHED.
backend/apps/chatwoot-my-tasks/index.html already contains a working beep()
(Web Audio), Notification.requestPermission() flow, toast() and 60-second
poll. helper/protonAlerts.js is that code, transcribed, with two changes the
new host requires: the toast container is created on demand rather than
relying on a static <div id="toasts">, and the notification helpers take an
options object. The my-tasks app keeps its own copies and its own SLA alerts
and is not modified by this patch or by anything backing it -- this is an
ADDITION. It fires on SLA warn/breach for a task list; this fires on new
inbound in the window the agent is actually working in. Trading a working
alert surface for a new one would be a net loss.

WHY THE FORK RATHER THAN THE IFRAME. A dashboard-app iframe is cross-origin
and sandboxed, is loaded only while the agent has that panel open, and cannot
know what the agent is looking at. Sidebar.vue is mounted on every dashboard
page, so installing from there makes alerting live wherever Chatwoot is. The
module renders into document.body (its own toast container and status strip)
rather than into Sidebar's template, which is why this patch adds no markup to
an upstream template at all.

THE EVENT SOURCE, AND WHY IT IS NOT A POLL. Chatwoot's ActionCable connector
writes pushed conversation/message events into the Vuex store -- that is how
the SPA's own conversation list updates without a refresh -- so watching the
store IS subscribing to the stream, with no second socket and no second
subscription to authenticate. A 60-second poll as the PRIMARY path would
notify an agent up to a minute after the customer's message is already visible
in their list, which is worse than no alert because it trains agents to ignore
it.

Only one store getter is verified by this repo's own already-merged patches
(`getSelectedChat`, used by 0055). The conversation-list getter is therefore a
CANDIDATE LIST (`CONVERSATION_GETTER_CANDIDATES`), and readConversations()
reports which one it found. If none matches, coverage is 'none', the module
goes to poll mode and SHOWS THE DEGRADED INDICATOR -- so a wrong guess here
degrades to a visibly-degraded 60-second poll, never to silence, and is fixed
by editing one array. That is stated here rather than hidden because it is the
single most likely thing about this patch to need a fix-up on the first real
build.

NEVER GO SILENT (task 3). Fallback is Chatwoot's own conversations API
(same-origin, the same devise_token_auth headers the SPA's own axios client
sends), polled on the same 60 s cadence the my-tasks app uses, plus a
degraded-mode strip at the bottom-left of the window. Two properties are
load-bearing:

  * Stream loss is concluded from EVIDENCE, never from silence. A quiet shift
    produces no evidence either way, and inventing a degraded state from
    silence would put a red strip on every quiet morning. The proof is the
    poll finding an inbound message older than STREAM_GRACE_MS (90 s) that the
    stream never delivered.
  * NO ALERT FIRES TWICE ON RECONNECT. Both paths claim message ids in one
    shared Set (claimAlert), so when the poll fires and the stream reconnects
    and both see the same message, whichever gets there first alerts and the
    other silently drops. A duplicate-alert storm on reconnect teaches agents
    to ignore alerts exactly as effectively as a beep every few seconds does.

NEW_INBOUND IS TOAST-ONLY BY DEFAULT, DELIBERATELY. Sound and desktop
notification exist for the event and are off. On a tenant where WhatsApp
carries most of the volume an audible alert per inbound message is a beep
every few seconds, and the first thing every agent does is switch off ALL
alerting -- including the sla_breach alerts that actually matter. The
requirement is met by the capability being present and per-agent
configurable, not by the loudest default. DEFAULT_ALERT_RULES matches
rules_store.py's seeded defaults, and the patch's tests assert this specific
default so that "improving" it to sound fails a test and makes the improver
read the reasoning.

AN AGENT'S OWN REPLY RAISES NOTHING, checked three ways in
isAlertableCustomerMessage: `private === true` (a private note),
`message_type !== 0` (1 is outgoing -- the agent's own reply and the bot's --
2 activity, 3 template), and `sender_type === 'User'` (a Chatwoot agent rather
than a contact). The third is DELIBERATELY REDUNDANT with the second: an
agent's own reply arriving back down the same stream is the classic
self-notification bug, and a redundant guard is what stops it if a future
channel ever delivers an agent-authored message with message_type 0.

DENIED NOTIFICATION PERMISSION IS SURFACED. A browser that has denied the
permission drops desktop alerts forever and silently, and Chrome will not
re-prompt after a denial. So when a rule asks for 'desktop' and the permission
is 'denied', the status strip appears with an "Enable notifications" button
that re-requests (which works from 'default') and, when the answer is still
'denied', says the only thing that can actually fix it: allow notifications
for the site in browser settings.

TASK 6, THE PREFERENCES PAGE. views/ProtonAlertPreferencesPage.vue carries
both halves of rules_router.py's permission split: every agent edits their own
six rows (`alerts.set_own_preferences`, granted to the default `agent` role),
and only `alerts.manage` can change an account default. Reset per row calls
DELETE /alerts/rules/mine/:event and repaints from the rule the router returns
after the reset, so an agent can get back to the inherited value without
guessing what it was. The nav entry is gated on
`alerts.set_own_preferences` rather than on an admin permission, because a
preferences page an agent cannot open leaves the per-agent override layer
unreachable by anyone -- the mistake ruling D5 and the
presence.set_own_status/translation.use decisions already had to correct.

api/protonAlerts.js reuses adminRequest() from protonAdmin.js rather than
adding a sixth request helper: adminRequest is the one that already forwards
the devise_token_auth access-token/client/uid triplet require_permission
needs (patch 0030 fixed exactly this for the earlier admin pages). agent_id is
sent on every /mine call because the router ignores it when RBAC is on and
400s without it when RBAC is off -- one form that works in both modes, and it
grants nothing in the mode that matters, since the router has no
"set another agent's preferences" capability at all.

TWO INDEPENDENT SWITCHES, STATED RATHER THAN ENGINEERED AROUND. The backend
setting `INBOUND_ALERTS_ENABLED` and this module's actual client-side gate are
NOT the same switch. The gate is `hasFeature('inbound_alerts')`, read from
window.__PROTON_CONFIG__.features -- a tenant's `PROTON_FEATURES` list, the
mechanism 0001/0002 established for `ai_assist`. There is no backend endpoint
that reports `inbound_alerts_enabled` to the frontend, and
deploy/tenants/*.env, docker-compose.tenant.yml and main.py were all
off-limits for these tasks, so an operator must add `inbound_alerts` to a
tenant's PROTON_FEATURES in addition to (not instead of) turning the backend
setting on. This is the same gap recorded for `FAQ_SUGGESTION_POPUP_ENABLED`
in register section 3g, and closing it is a deploy-config task.

The PREFERENCES PAGE is different and does have a single real switch: it is
gated by RBAC (a real backend-derived permission) and reports
`ALERT_RULES_ENABLED` honestly, because rules_router.py answers
{"disabled": true, "reason": ...} on every endpoint when that setting is off
and the page renders that reason instead of an empty table.

KNOWN NARROWING GAP. `my_inbox` and `my_team` scopes FAIL OPEN when the
viewer's membership lists are unknown: every conversation reaching this code
has already passed Chatwoot's own inbox-visibility rules, so "an inbox I can
see" is the wider filter, and alerting nothing because a membership lookup came
back empty is precisely the failure this package exists to avoid. `teamIds` is
always empty today (no verified store getter for it), so `my_team` currently
behaves as `all` for a given agent. `mine` and `all` are exact. An
UNRECOGNISED scope alerts NOTHING -- a typo must not quietly widen a rule.

DEPENDENCIES / STACKING. This patch stacks on 0025 (Sidebar.vue's
`useProtonPermissions` import at line 15 and its `protonHasPermission` setup
line at 68 are 0025's own added lines, transcribed verbatim from its merged
diff) and on 0043 (the Cases nav block, whose tail is the nav hunk's leading
context) and 0003 (`...inboxRoutes,` in dashboard.routes.js). It anchors on
0043's content rather than on 0053's or 0054's DELIBERATELY: 0053/0054 are the
two least-verified patches in the series, and anchoring below their insertions
means a change to their CONTENT cannot break this patch's context lines --
only their line counts shift, which git apply absorbs as an offset. Line
numbers were computed from 0054's post-image (its Sidebar hunk at old line 647
adding 16 new lines, and its routes hunk at old line 85 adding 15) forward
through 0035's, 0041's and 0043's known block lengths; if 0053/0054 need a
line-number fix-up this patch may need the same offset, but not a content
change.

