BOTH FORMERLY-UNVERIFIED GUESSES ARE NOW CONFIRMED (2026-08-11), read out of
`chatwoot/chatwoot:v4.15.1` itself rather than guessed, along with the whole
59-patch stack applying in order in a throwaway container:

- The getters are right, and it is the FIRST candidate that hits, so the
  healthy `coverage: 'list'` path is what actually runs. `store/modules/
  conversations/getters.js` defines `getAllConversations`, and that module is
  NOT namespaced (`export default { state, getters, actions, mutations }`), so
  it resolves at the root. The second candidate is dead code, kept as
  documentation. `INBOX_GETTER_CANDIDATES` is right for the opposite reason:
  the inboxes module IS `namespaced: true`, so `inboxes/getInboxes` is live.
- The response shape is right, and it is the first branch:
  `index.json.jbuilder` is `json.data { json.meta …; json.payload … }`, so
  `body.data.payload` is the array.

TWO THINGS THE FIRST DRAFT GOT WRONG, FIXED IN THE HUNKS BELOW:

1. `sort_by=last_activity_at` is not a valid key. `SORT_OPTIONS` has
   `last_activity_at_asc`/`_desc`; an unknown key silently falls through to
   `SORT_OPTIONS['last_activity_at_desc']`, which happens to be the order we
   want. It worked by luck, not by contract. Now sends `_desc` explicitly.
2. The poll was capped at 25 conversations. `ConversationFinder#conversations`
   branches: with `updated_within` present it returns the filtered relation and
   never calls `.page(…).per(…)`; without it, page size is the
   `CONVERSATION_RESULTS_PER_PAGE` env var (default 25) and no `page` param was
   sent. `updated_within=600` removes the cap without a paging loop and bounds
   the result by recent activity, which is what a staleness watchdog wants.
   There is no `per_page` param, so this is the only way to lift that cap from
   a query string.

WHAT THE POLL STILL CANNOT SEE, stated rather than implied. The conversation
partial emits `json.messages [ …messages.last… ]` — an array of exactly ONE.
So the fallback sees each conversation's most recent message only. An
unanswered inbound IS the most recent message, which is the alert condition,
so this is sufficient for its purpose — but it is not full coverage, and an
inbound followed by any later message drops out of view. `set_inboxes` also
scopes results to `current_user.assigned_inboxes`, which is the correct
boundary for an agent-facing alert. Both fail toward a visible degraded
indicator rather than toward silence.

